EXPERIMENT 7 – PRIME NUMBER CHECKING USING JUNIT

AIM
To write JUnit test cases to check whether a number is prime and validate the output using JUnit assertions.

ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the JUnit 5 dependency.
2. Create a class named PrimeUtil.
3. Write the isPrime() method to check whether a number is prime.
4. Create PrimeUtilTest with prime and composite number test cases.
5. Use assertTrue() and assertFalse() to verify the results.
6. Run the tests using mvn test.
CODE
PrimeUtil.java
public class PrimeUtil {
    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}


PrimeUtilTest.java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class PrimeUtilTest {
    @Test
    void primeNumbers() {
        assertTrue(PrimeUtil.isPrime(2));
        assertTrue(PrimeUtil.isPrime(3));
        assertTrue(PrimeUtil.isPrime(17));
    }

    @Test
    void compositeNumbers() {
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(4));
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(25));
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(100));
    }

    @Test
    void numbersLessThanTwo() {
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(1));
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(0));
        assertFalse(PrimeUtil.isPrime(-7));
    }
}


